From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] PinnerService: prefetch the last used app once the user is unlocked

0025 read the last used app's compiled code at PHASE_BOOT_COMPLETED. On the
device (Test V40) it never read anything, and said nothing: boot completes
about half a second before the system user is unlocked, and until then the
user's recent tasks are not loaded (getRecentTasks returns none for a locked
user) and home resolves to Settings' FallbackHome, so no app was found.

The prefetch now starts on ACTION_USER_UNLOCKED for the system user, which is
sent after the recent tasks are loaded. The FallbackHome task, still listed
then, is skipped like home (its intent has CATEGORY_HOME), and a prefetch that
finds nothing to read says so in the log.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/PinnerService.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/PinnerService.java
@@ -130,10 +130,24 @@
 
     @Override
     public void onBootPhase(int phase) {
-        if (phase == SystemService.PHASE_BOOT_COMPLETED
+        // Once the system user is unlocked. Boot completes before that, while its recent tasks
+        // are not loaded yet and home is Settings' FallbackHome: at PHASE_BOOT_COMPLETED
+        // nothing was found to read (PW3 Test V40).
+        if (phase == SystemService.PHASE_SYSTEM_SERVICES_READY
                 && SystemProperties.getBoolean(PREFETCH_RECENT_APP_PROPERTY, false)) {
-            // Its own thread: the reads take seconds and must not hold up the pinner handler.
-            new Thread(this::prefetchRecentApp, "PinnerPrefetch").start();
+            mContext.registerReceiver(new BroadcastReceiver() {
+                @Override
+                public void onReceive(Context context, Intent intent) {
+                    if (intent.getIntExtra(Intent.EXTRA_USER_HANDLE, UserHandle.USER_NULL)
+                            != UserHandle.USER_SYSTEM) {
+                        return;
+                    }
+                    context.unregisterReceiver(this);
+                    // Its own thread: the reads take seconds and must not hold up the
+                    // pinner handler.
+                    new Thread(PinnerService.this::prefetchRecentApp, "PinnerPrefetch").start();
+                }
+            }, new IntentFilter(Intent.ACTION_USER_UNLOCKED));
         }
     }
 
